<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bight of Benin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> is a bay of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a> on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, stretching roughly from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-delta">Niger Delta</a>. In the era of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> it was known as the Slave Coast. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>'s military pressure on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> shaped the politics of the whole Bight of Benin coast, and many enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> were shipped from its ports to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bight-of-benin.jpg" alt="Bight of Benin" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gulf_of_Guinea_(English).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their trade linked Dagbon with the Fezzan, Egypt and the <b>Bight of Benin</b>, and the kingdom exchanged salt and kola nuts with the Mossi kingdoms, the Mali and Songhai empires and the Hausa Bakwai.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dagbon">Kingdom of Dagbon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Hundreds of thousands of people, many of them captives of Dahomean wars, left the <b>Bight of Benin</b> through Ouidah and neighbouring ports, and their religious traditions shaped the Americas: the vodun of the Fon and their neighbours is a principal root of Haitian Vodou and of the Tambor…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Regionally, Oyo’s sustained pressure on Dahomey shaped the political development of the entire <b>Bight of Benin</b> coast, while the empire’s collapse produced massive population displacements that transformed the demographic and political geography of what is now south-western Nigeria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Lagos became the chief port of the <b>Bight of Benin</b>, exporting palm oil and later cocoa.</blockquote><a href="/state/lagos-colony">Lagos Colony</a></li></ul></section>
